WEBVTT

00:00:09.235 --> 00:00:11.890
<v><b>Presenter:</b></v> Good afternoon, and welcome to the programme.

00:00:12.329 --> 00:00:16.675
Now, we all know that parents take different approaches to bringing up their kids,

00:00:16.966 --> 00:00:20.136
and, thinking of our own upbringing, and people we know,

00:00:20.197 --> 00:00:24.529
we all probably have an idea about which styles of parenting we approve of.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:29.468
We’ve been hearing a lot in the media recently about different parenting styles

00:00:29.777 --> 00:00:31.904
– for example, ‘free-range’ parenting,

00:00:32.216 --> 00:00:35.742
where children have lots of freedom with not much supervision,

00:00:36.053 --> 00:00:42.996
or ‘tiger’ parenting, where parents are very demanding and put a lot of pressure on children to achieve.

00:00:43.625 --> 00:00:44.440
In particular,

00:00:44.465 --> 00:00:50.686
there’s been a lot of attention given recently to the phenomenon of so-called ‘helicopter parents’

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:55.212
– the ones who hover over their children and control their every waking moment

00:00:55.509 --> 00:00:58.835
– and this is generally regarded as a negative thing.

00:00:59.307 --> 00:01:03.004
Joining us today is an expert on the psychology of parenting,

00:01:03.267 --> 00:01:07.818
who’s going to discuss a new book called <i>Love, Money, and Parenting</i>,

00:01:08.112 --> 00:01:12.629
written by economists Matthias Doepke and Fabrizio Zilibotti.

00:01:13.014 --> 00:01:18.273
It argues, controversially, that, in fact, in the UK and the USA,

00:01:18.538 --> 00:01:24.467
the children of helicopter parents do better at school, are more focused and conscientious,

00:01:24.758 --> 00:01:28.348
and are less likely to take risks with drink and drugs.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.769
Hello, Janice, and thank you for coming on the programme.

00:01:31.794 --> 00:01:32.639
<v><b>Janice:</b></v> Hello.